n the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-4x.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b008b94eebbd24ae51670447848ce2d644c067d43dd92d72de85b048358cc54.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497748 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497762 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-3x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9992e41-e63d-5336-a605-48b1d684791a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497762 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-3x.$ \n\n![](images/08/08e854b04d47ff670b197a2a0c7b51c13eb135626e0ed6cdfd24368d4032f259.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497756 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497764 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-1x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffd546da-09fc-5649-b60d-749bd09256a6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497764 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1x \\\\  & =-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1x \\\\  & =-1(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1x.$ \n\n![](images/73/7322801cf231391f58d39f2fad42b779e04fc1b81b686bcb3291ebfee5651226.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497765 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186054695.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497765 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "5acf3299-1ee4-5045-8a1b-4834747495c0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497782 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497774 #edb/ref 1071 "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x-3.$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a903cf9-42ce-5563-9b36-303a8f1c1320" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497774 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x-3.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=-(0)-3=-3,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)-3=-4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-(2)-3=-5,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)-3=-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c9/c96b7da7b073986de68a557df8a9dbde16d39566c87eb0b5ba6ac62bd40da7c4.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x-3.$\n\n![](images/05/05d10ee3d2e6bc295a7d2e6b07ca726c9c11c159d74f1d81c71fce761fa4275e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497771 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497775 #edb/ref 1071 "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5da1545d-7bf4-538f-94f3-464d21187a82" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497775 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x-6.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)-6=-6,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)-6=-4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)-6=-2,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)-6=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/95/958c5a848a09e43832324b8afb000348c7630cd2c42e99a51215d48d9895a00e.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x-6.$\n\n![](images/3e/3efb14f33400cd4a6353b93e5d99a826083cca6859785d5bd5d837fb00ad302f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497776 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497777 #edb/ref 1071 "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67365bc6-453f-5a19-82a4-9817c6e05a99" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497777 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x-6.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=(0)-6=-6,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=(1)-6=-5,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=(2)-6=-4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=(3)-6=-3.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-3$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/99/99188f2a0f8de0054a6375a88a11242bea7e4799cc17552cd9ba0dd00d5a0788.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=x-6.$\n\n![](images/44/44b8f095ef9aca54e6215b1b61c9fad562dd54b0f00b7e9b8e2efdd904dd83e6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497787 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186054694.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186497787 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "9ae4d764-bdf7-5b4d-982a-b379ea720263" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498716 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498666 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498666 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4c714262b9da22ea9ad859817db86a3aaf0b29ef811246aedc969bc9999c0348.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x+3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f863f32-c463-5f07-9482-1c6f6e74c019" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498666 #edb/ref 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,0)$, $B(-1,4)$ and $C(3,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (0) & =1(-3)+3 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (4) & =1(-1)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (6) & =1(3)+3 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498667 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3cee718f18c9ef081b3e90a70d4db00ef0339d7d5f0b87423f7c5687709471a6.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12a60b9c-85ce-50af-b45d-06caed36a198" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498667 #edb/ref 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,9),B(-1,4)$ and $C(1,-7).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (9) & =-5(-2)-2 \\\\ 9 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (4) & =-5(-1)-2 \\\\ 4 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,-7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (-7) & =-5(1)-2 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498715 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498669 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498669 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/70/70b04ef6ee89c2bf06db99444f1f89afbe2ea15871f8f79da29668f7be3f9f97.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21a9c497-cf26-5338-8f2e-52de8c6489fc" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186498669 #edb/ref 1074 "The 