s an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(6,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{2}{3}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{2}{3}(6)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4+4 \\\\ 0 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501334 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501329 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/87/87cbbd5a53079bb2cd744e1af18e901f6e54239a0fc6330984659c75cbdd3509.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=9$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+9$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3d6f732-ed9f-51fe-87bd-f9f387742c79" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501329 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,9)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/c3/c3a0113d32377bd9bac1569081dce1caad04e1cd385805fbb225c3964e7f85d7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=9.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9)+3(0) & =9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(3) & =9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=9$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(9)+(0) & =3 \\\\ 3+0 & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(3) & =3 \\\\ 0+3 & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+9.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9) & =-3(0)+9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(3)+9 \\\\ 0 & =-9+9 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+9$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501335 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501322 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501335 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501335 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f0218ba02ef7305b1f22582379d15e8da24c859a239b45130c053aa5c3f8cde1.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-3x+\\frac{3}{8}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{3}{8}x+3$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff7154d8-1178-59c1-89cf-d6727039f41b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186501335 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,3)$ and $(8,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/f1/f193f5ea70aa459427c5c4abecfdb74c3e12c6de4e22522d71f5ca1f6b1b9cc1.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-3x+\\frac{3}{8}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3) & =-3(0)+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}